Major Depressive Episode
Young Adult & Adolecence
Pervasive depressed mood, Anhedonia, Pervasive fatigue, Feelings of worthlessness

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-027
Reason for Visit: Acute psychological decompensation with severe psychomotor retardation, complete cessation of oral intake, and expressions of profound worthlessness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Flatmate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a patient who has been entirely bedbound for 72 hours, refusing all food and fluids, and expressing severe, passive suicidal ideation. An urgent after-hours physical and mental state examination was strictly required to assess acute suicide risk, exclude severe organic medical causes for the sudden functional collapse (e.g., severe hypothyroidism or systemic infection), and establish an immediate safety and rehydration plan prior to morning psychiatric triage.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I am completely empty, I cannot move my body, and everyone would be better off if I just went to sleep and didn't wake up."
History of Present Illness: Over the preceding six weeks, the patient has experienced a pervasive depressed mood most of the day, nearly every day. The flatmate reports the patient has exhibited markedly diminished interest or pleasure in all previously enjoyed activities (anhedonia). Over the last two weeks, there has been significant unintentional weight loss. The patient reports severe insomnia, followed by days of hypersomnia. The current acute crisis is characterized by severe psychomotor retardation, pervasive fatigue, and a total loss of energy, rendering the patient unable to rise from bed. The patient expresses intense feelings of worthlessness and excessive, inappropriate guilt regarding their inability to function. There is a severely diminished ability to think or concentrate. The flatmate requested the urgent visit due to the patient verbalizing recurrent thoughts of death and passive suicidal ideation tonight.
Functional Status: Bedbound; entirely dependent on the flatmate for basic hygiene and attempted oral intake.
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute risk for self-harm and severe dehydration; flatmate is overwhelmed and unable to manage the medical/psychiatric risk independently.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/68 | HR: 65 | Temp: 36.4 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Patient demonstrates profound psychomotor retardation, lying rigidly in bed, maintaining zero eye contact, with a flat, blunted affect and delayed speech latency.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination is grossly intact with no focal deficits. Thyroid gland is not palpable. Cardiovascular and respiratory systems are unremarkable. Skin turgor is slightly diminished, indicating early mild dehydration. Mental State Examination (MSE) reveals a severely depressed, restricted affect, slow and monotonous speech, and morbid thought content. The patient admits to passive death wishes but denies an active, formulated plan or immediate intent for self-harm during the consultation. The presentation unequivocally aligns with the diagnostic tetrad of a pervasive depressed mood, anhedonia, pervasive fatigue, and feelings of worthlessness.
Environmental Observations: The room is dark, curtains drawn, with untouched meals accumulating on the bedside table. No obvious sharps or stockpiled medications are visible.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Major Depressive Episode without psychotic features - Status: Acute Crisis
Differentials:

Hypothyroidism - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary acute driver due to the absence of physical signs such as a goitre, cold intolerance, or severe bradycardia, though baseline TFTs must be checked.

Substance-Induced Mood Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely based on a negative collateral history from the flatmate, absence of physical stigmata of substance use, and lack of physiological withdrawal signs.

Bipolar Affective Disorder (Depressed Phase) - Ruled out/unlikely based on the current history lacking any prior episodes of mania or hypomania, though longitudinal psychiatric assessment is required.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Requires continuous supervision and urgent psychiatric intervention)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is in the midst of a severe major depressive episode complicated by profound psychomotor retardation and passive suicidal ideation, leading to an acute cessation of basic life-sustaining activities (eating/drinking). The expression of a desire to die necessitated an immediate, after-hours psychiatric risk assessment to determine if involuntary emergency department transfer was mandated. Clinical assessment confirmed extremely high distress and functional collapse, but the lack of immediate active intent allowed for safe, highly-structured community containment tonight, provided the flatmate acts as a continuous safety net. Delaying this assessment would have left a high-risk patient unassessed, posing a critical risk of a fatal self-harm event or severe dehydration overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Conducted a comprehensive safety and risk assessment. Mandated immediate environmental lockdown by the flatmate (securing all sharps, medications, and household chemicals). Supervised the intake of 250ml of oral rehydration fluid. Ordered baseline bloods (FBC, TFTs, B12, Vit D) and provided the PHQ-9 questionnaire for the GP follow-up. Initiated an urgent referral to the local Acute Care Mental Health Team for review tomorrow.
Positive Behavioral Change: To safely break the physical inertia, sit upright on the edge of the bed for exactly two minutes every hour while awake, directly proving to the brain that voluntary motor control is still possible despite the profound fatigue. To prevent severe dehydration, take one small sip of water every time the flatmate enters the room, utilizing an external trigger to guarantee basic fluid intake when internal motivation is completely absent.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral sent to community Acute Psychiatric Triage.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory, urgent review by the regular GP tomorrow morning to initiate a Mental Health Treatment Plan and coordinate with the acute psychiatric team.

